{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the configuration-contract repair at exact clean pushed head 5f2251157cdc0983500b6f0f41c8dfadc8428148 on request/repo-remote-link. Checkout clean, origin matching, base c679fb73.\n\nYour verdict d2245525 is ratified at 8f51a2b8 and this answers it. Your simplification is what got built: stop forwarding caller-selected configuration files, give the constructor an explicit minimal trusted contract.\n\nA FOURTH VECTOR I FOUND WHILE CHECKING THE FIX, AND IT CHANGES THE SHAPE OF YOUR FINDING. Dropping the whole GIT_CONFIG family is not sufficient. HOME is inherited by design, so a caller who sets only HOME reaches ~/.gitconfig and gets the same execution. I verified it with no GIT_CONFIG variable set at all: the marker was created; with the pins this head sets, it is not. So the pins are part of the security bound rather than borrowed isolation, and the obvious fix — drop the family and stop — would have left the hole open through a variable neither of us named. Please check whether I have now missed a fifth: XDG_CONFIG_HOME is the one I would look at, and I would rather you tell me than me tell you.\n\nTHE CONTRACT. Every inherited name beginning with GIT_CONFIG is dropped by prefix, so a future member is refused by default rather than admitted by omission. gitTrustedConfiguration then sets GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null, appended last so the exec package's last-value-wins makes it a statement rather than a hope. The repository's own file remains, because remote.origin.url lives there and reading it is the point. \"The repository's configuration and nothing else\", not \"no configuration\".\n\nWHAT IS GIVEN UP, and I would like this judged rather than waved through. safe.directory is now honoured only from protected configuration, so these commands refuse a repository owned by another user outright instead of consulting an operator allowance. That surfaces as a visible failure — LocalWorktrees errors, citingDocuments turns it into a refused retirement — not a wrong answer. I checked what depends on it rather than assuming: nothing in the repository references safe.directory except the new comment and the architecture page, and the suite is green including internal/docset, whose documented-example runner deliberately sets a real GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL. If you think a deployment serving a repository it does not own is a real case, this trade is the thing to push back on.\n\nONE TEST CHANGED ITS BREAKAGE CHANNEL AND I WANT THE REASONING CHECKED. TestRetirementIsRefusedWhenTheCitationLookupCannotRun broke the lookup through GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, which this head closes. It now writes grep.threads = -1 into the repository's own configuration file, because that channel is inside the contract by necessity and so cannot be closed by a later environment bound — keeping it a test of the exit-code distinction rather than a second test of the configuration bound. Its baseline half still proves the refusal is not the ordinary citation refusal in disguise.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched, tree restored after each. Removing the configuration bound fails the new regression's GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL vector, marker present and checkout reported dirty. Dropping the family without setting the pins fails its HOME vector — the row that proves the pins are load-bearing. Removing the exit-code distinction so failures read as \"found nothing\" fails TestRetirementIsRefusedWhenTheCitationLookupCannotRun; making every failure a refusal fails TestCitationLookupFindingNothingStillPermitsTheRetirement too. The new regression carries control runs outside the constructor that fail hard if the exploit stops reproducing, so a green result cannot be vacuous.\n\nDOCUMENTATION. The architecture page records the configuration bound as a mechanism, beside the routing-variable mechanism, and keeps the citation guard as a property per planner's ruling. One stale example is corrected there and in a test comment: a hostile command-scope configuration is no longer reachable, so the list of things that can stop the lookup now reads a broken Git, a resource limit, or a setting in the repository's own configuration file.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 227.430s, internal/app 160.072s, internal/kernel 156.489s, internal/docset 103.170s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 92.845s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Independent review of exact clean pushed head 5f2251157cdc0983500b6f0f41c8dfadc8428148 on request/repo-remote-link, citing all seven live artifacts at that head: 1e551b60 internal/app, 645bbfa9 internal/service, b48a67a3 ui, fcbd251b internal/service/uidist, de1ebb98 docs/reference/architecture.md, abbb427f docs/reference/gs/serve.md, 9501939a docs/concepts/components.md. Governing request is planner's repair child 6631aeeb; my promise is d38a434a. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions and file the verdict with gs review resting on all seven artifacts. SECURITY: judge whether any inherited value can still carry an execution-affecting directive into a Git command this package runs, whether the stated pins actually hold against every inherited channel including HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME, whether the fail-closed citation rule survives the change, and whether the safe.directory trade is stated honestly. Do not merge.","head":"5f2251157cdc0983500b6f0f41c8dfadc8428148","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}